# caddy3 -- reverse proxy
#
# `reverse_proxy` forwards requests to an upstream and relays the answer.
# The practice API (`opslings target`) plays the upstream; its address comes
# in through $UPSTREAM, e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080.
#
# Path matchers are exact unless they end in `*`:
#   /api      matches only /api
#   /api/*    matches /api/ and everything below it
#
# Directive order is NOT the order in the file: Caddy sorts directives by a
# built-in list (see "directive order" in the docs). `handle` blocks make
# routing explicit when that sorting gets in your way.
#
# `header` edits *response* headers: `header Name value` sets one,
# `header -Name` deletes one.
#
# Task, on $PORT:
#   /api/...        proxied to $UPSTREAM (path unchanged)
#   anything else   200 "edge ok", answered by Caddy itself
#   every response carries  X-Served-By: opslings-edge
#   and no `Server` header (why advertise what you run?)
#
#   opslings target --port 8080
#   env PORT=8083 UPSTREAM=http://127.0.0.1:8080 caddy run --config Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#   curl -i localhost:8083/api/items
#

{
	admin off
}

:{$PORT} {
	header X-Served-By opslings-edge
	header -Server

	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy {$UPSTREAM}
	}

	handle {
		respond "edge ok" 200
	}
}
